 were located inside the glovebox near the fire source to avoid losses through the sampling lines; the rest were located just outside the glovebox. Instruments for smoke particle characterization included a Tapered Element Oscillating Microbalance Personal Dust Monitor (TEOM PDM) and a TSI Dust Trak DRX to measure particle mass concentration, a TSI PTrak for number concentration and a thermal precipitator for collection of particles for microscopic analysis. Materials studied included Nomex®, M22759 wire insulation, granulated circuit board, polyvinyl chloride (PVC), Polytetrafluoroethylene (PTFE), Kapton®, and mixtures of PTFE and Kapton®. Furnace temperatures ranged from 340o to 640o C, focusing on the smoldering regime. Of particular interest in these tests was confirming burn repeatability and production of acid gases with different fuel mixture compositions, as well as the dependence of aerosol concentrations on temperature.","isLessonsLearned":false,"disseminated":"DOCUMENT_AND_METADATA","meetings":[{"submissionId":20140002231,"name":"American Inst. of Aeronautics and Astronautics","location":"Reston, VA, United States","id":"0a8b37e729f84f4aaa96352f2fb27a06"},{"country":"United States","submissionId":20140002231,"endDate":"2013-07-18T00:00:00.0000000+00:00","sponsors":[{"meta":{"organization":{"name":"American Inst. of Aeronautics and Astronautics","location":"Reston, VA, United States"}},"meetingId":"fd8745a4e0fa48b0a5b96e85850a620e","id":"a2098a5963804f20b9d4aa5ae89ea416"}],"name":"International Conference on Environmental Systems","location":"Vail, CO","id":"fd8745a4e0fa48b0a5b96e85850a620e","startDate":"2013-07-14T00:00:00.0000000+00:00"}],"publications":[{"submissionId":20140002231,"id":"760ae221e0644587b7a5f6ddf1450205","publicationDate":"2013-07-14T00:00:00.0000000+00:00"}],"status":"CURATED","related":[{"disseminated":"DOCUMENT_AND_METADATA","id":20140011015,"type":"SEE_ALSO","title":"Materials Combustion Testing and Combustion Product Sensor Evaluations in FY12","stiType":"PRESENTATION","distribution":"PUBLIC","status":"CURATED"},{"disseminated":"DOCUMENT_AND_METADATA","id":20140011015,"type":"SEE_ALSO","title":"Materials Combustion Testing and Combustion Product Sensor Evaluations in FY12","stiType":"PRESENTATION","distribution":"PUBLIC","status":"CURATED"}],"downloads":[{"draft":false,"mimetype":"application/pdf","name":"20140002231.pdf","type":"STI","links":{"original":"/api/citations/20140002231/downloads/20140002231.pdf","pdf":"/api/citations/20140002231/downloads/20140002231.pdf","fulltext":"/api/citations/20140002231/downloads/20140002231.txt"}}],"downloadsAvailable":true,"index":"submissions-2026-11-12-06-24"},{"_meta":{"score":148.04848},"copyright":{"thirdPartyPermissionsProduced":false,"disclosedToPublic":false,"containsIndication":false,"publisherPermissionOrRightsToDistribute":false,"belongsToUsGov":false,"determinationType":"OTHER","thirdPartyContentCondition":"NOT_SET","belongsToContractor":false,"disclosedInvention":false,"submissionId":20160008254,"containsThirdPartyMaterial":false,"belongsToPublisher":false,"id":"560682541289460bb26c582e20cc5b27","belongsToAuthors":false},"subjectCategories":["Inorganic, Organic And Physical Chemistry","Instrumentation And Photography"],"exportControl":{"isExportControl":"NO","submissionId":20160008254,"ear":"NO","id":"b1f5fea18f8b40148b8e1b405057b8c3","itar":"NO"},"distributionDate":"2019-07-11T00:00:00.0000000+00:00","otherReportNumbers":["ICES-2014-244","Report Number: ICES-2014-244"],"title":"Five-Channel Infrared Laser Absorption Spectrometer for Combustion Product Monitoring Aboard Manned Spacecraft","stiType":"CONFERENCE_PAPER","distribution":"PUBLIC","submittedDate":"2016-07-01T08:07:06.4000000+00:00","authorAffiliations":[{"sequence":0,"submissionId":20160008254,"meta":{"author":{"name":"Briggs, Ryan M."},"organization":{"name":"Jet Propulsion Lab., California Inst. of Tech.","location":"Pasadena, CA, United States"}},"id":"bd2f28bc898f4be4b5c482f1a1505d0a"},{"sequence":1,"submissionId":20160008254,"meta":{"author":{"name":"Frez, Clifford"},"organization":{"name":"Jet Propulsion Lab., California Inst. of Tech.","location":"Pasadena, CA, United States"}},"id":"1dd02b07f7d940dd96f8b0cd75392eb7"},{"sequence":2,"submissionId":20160008254,"meta":{"author":{"name":"Borgentun, Carl E."},"organization":{"name":"Jet Propulsion Lab., California Inst. of Tech.","location":"Pasadena, CA, United States"}},"id":"4a13f4f9e0b541be82e8d0caa28ead27"},{"sequence":3,"submissionId":20160008254,"meta":{"author":{"name":"Bagheri, Mahmood"},"organization":{"name":"Jet Propulsion Lab., California Inst. of Tech.","location":"Pasadena, CA, United States"}},"id":"d4a4b77b20fc45a8a2350a8ab48aeb12"},{"sequence":4,"submissionId":20160008254,"meta":{"author":{"name":"Forouhar, Siamak"},"organization":{"name":"Jet Propulsion Lab., California Inst. of Tech.","location":"Pasadena, CA, United States"}},"id":"48cafcbb46a24d9696be80f3f54d275b"},{"sequence":5,"submissionId":20160008254,"meta":{"author":{"name":"May, Randy D."},"organization":{"name":"Port City Instruments, LLC","location":"Wilmington, NC, United States"}},"id":"3530ff1e262345449ee648f783cead55"}],"stiTypeDetails":"Conference Paper","technicalReviewType":"TECHNICAL_REVIEW_TYPE_NONE","modified":"2025-08-31T18:39:21.7150190+00:00","id":20160008254,"sourceIdentifiers":[{"number":"hdl:2014/45610","submissionId":20160008254,"id":"055a0f5959454a0c98015f4c03240e92","type":"HANDLE"},{"submissionId":20160008254,"id":"a71ce83466df40d2b477c26c6426f46c","descriptor":"hdl:2014/45610"}],"legacyMeta":{"__type":"LegacyMetaIndex, StrivesApi.ServiceModel","accessionNumber":""},"created":"2016-07-01T08:07:06.4000000+00:00","center":{"code":"JPL","name":"Jet Propulsion Laboratory","id":"efbc9c123a0f47caafa95d99b7d2e1a1"},"onlyAbstract":false,"sensitiveInformation":2,"abstract":"Continuous combustion product monitoring aboard manned spacecraft can prevent chronic exposure to hazardous compounds and also provides early detection of combustion events. As future missions extend beyond low-Earth orbit, analysis of returned environmental samples becomes impractical and safety monitoring should be performed in situ. Here, we describe initial designs of a five-channel tunable laser absorption spectrometer to continuously monitor combustion products with the goal of minimal maintenance and calibration over long-duration missions. The instrument incorporates dedicated laser channels to simultaneously target strong mid-infrared absorption lines of CO, HCl, HCN, HF, and CO2. The availability of low-power-consumption semiconductor lasers operating in the 2 to 5 micron wavelength range affords the flexibility to select absorption lines for each gas with maximum interaction strength and minimal interference from other gases, which enables the design of a compact and mechanically robust spectrometer with low-level sensitivity. In this paper, we focus primarily on absorption line selection based on the availability of low-power single-mode semiconductor laser sources designed specifically for the target wavelength range.","isLessonsLearned":false,"disseminated":"METADATA_ONLY","meetings":[{"submissionId":20160008254,"name":"International Conference On Environmental Systems, Inc. (ICES)","location":"Stafford Springs, CT, United States","id":"006cd36ef3dc4f93ad459ec3d1122228"},{"submissionId":20160008254,"name":"American Society of Mechanical Engineers","location":"New York, NY, United States","id":"7df27690f4a14724a4d568501d4faa61"},{"submissionId":20160008254,"name":"American Inst. of Chemical Engineers","location":"New York, NY, United States","id":"0e53ab2b7e084cf586fb2f23977bacd1"},{"country":"United States","submissionId":20160008254,"endDate":"2014-07-17T00:00:00.0000000+00:00","sponsors":[{"organizationId":"9381b76034b1432b8f4124cf06b3d774","meta":{"organization":{"name":"International Conference On Environmental Systems, Inc. (ICES)","location":"Stafford Springs, CT, United States"}},"meetingId":"aaa9fe2f51184a5da38e578d75efd239","id":"efb19d56fdf348558afac242fa322670"},{"meta":{"organization":{"name":"American Society of Mechanical Engineers","location":"New York, NY, United States"}},"meetingId":"aaa9fe2f51184a5da38e578d75efd239","id":"2082960ce6eb43c0ac5f1d849146070f"},{"meta":{"organization":{"name":"Texas Tech Univ.","location":"Lubbock, TX, United States"}},"meetingId":"aaa9fe2f51184a5da38e578d75efd239","id":"8fe4aa8b62134e128fe1bfc9ee9a3ba5"},{"meta":{"organization":{"name":"American Inst. of Chemical Engineers","location":"New York, NY, United States"}},"meetingId":"aaa9fe2f51184a5da38e578d75efd239","id":"4589d4d907284792aea45a78408bddb9"}],"name":"International Conference on Environmental Systems (ICES)","location":"Tucson, AZ","id":"aaa9fe2f51184a5da38e578d75efd239","startDate":"2014-07-13T00:00:00.0000000+00:00"}],"publications":[{"submissionId":20160008254,"id":"ecf752cbdfc1443797dc9117b9e8c188","publicationDate":"2014-07-13T00:00:00.0000000+00:00"}],"status":"CURATED","related":[],"downloads":[],"downloadsAvailable":false,"index":"submissions-2026-11-12-06-24"},{"_meta":{"score":101.44757},"copyright":{"thirdPartyPermissionsProduced":false,"disclosedToPublic":false,"approverComments":"Items in TRS are protected by copyright, but are furnished with U.S. government purpose use rights.","containsIndication":true,"publisherPermissionOrRightsToDistribute":false,"belongsToUsGov":false,"determinationType":"OTHER","thirdPartyContentCondition":"NOT_SET","belongsToContractor":false,"disclosedInvention":false,"submissionId":20220001449,"containsThirdPartyMaterial":false,"belongsToPublisher":true,"id":"775f17d5f4954436a55397f6bbeff4ac","belongsToAuthors":false},"exportControl":{"isExportControl":"UNSET","submissionId":20220001449,"ear":"UNSET","id":"cb61646ed86f431588eee949551912b7","itar":"UNSET"},"created":"2020-07-12T00:00:00.0000000+00:00","distributionDate":"2020-07-12T00:00:00.0000000+00:00","center":{"code":"JPL","name":"Jet Propulsion Laboratory","id":"efbc9c123a0f47caafa95d99b7d2e1a1"},"onlyAbstract":false,"sensitiveInformation":0,"abstract":"A miniaturized tunable laser absorption spectrometer capable of measuring gas-phase concentrations of CO, CO2, and O2 has been developed for operation in spacecraft environments. As part of the Spacecraft Fire Safety Demonstration (Saffire) project, we previously demonstrated the Combustion Product Monitor (CPM) instrument to characterize pyrolysis of relevant test materials in low gravity aboard a transfer vehicle returning from low Earth orbit. Here, we adapted the CPM instrument design to achieve detection of CO, CO2, and O2 with improved minimum detection limits and dynamic range as well as reduced instrument size and power. The instrument can be operated in either an open or sealed configureation, allowing for ambient gas composition measuremnts or in-line monitoring of gas streams. By accurately monitoring CO, CO2, and O2 with broad pressure/temperature calibration and response time on the order of 1 s, the instrument can be used to characterize the dynamics of combustion reactions in a variety of environments.","title":"Miniaturized Laser Absorption Spectrometer for Combustion Calorimetry and Process Monitoring","stiType":"PREPRINT","distribution":"PUBLIC","authorAffiliations":[{"sequence":0,"submissionId":20220001449,"meta":{"author":{"name":"Briggs, Ryan M."}},"id":"c562fd2950d742eb8799c825a6a26cfc"},{"sequence":0,"submissionId":20220001449,"meta":{"author":{"name":"Tallarida, Nicholas R."}},"id":"b5a705d0be8c40e1a5b35082e5d60d5f"},{"sequence":0,"submissionId":20220001449,"meta":{"author":{"name":"Fradet, Mathieu"}},"id":"b4f733b9300c43d09ef680976a1e503b"}],"disseminated":"METADATA_ONLY","stiTypeDetails":"Preprint (Draft being sent to journal)","technicalReviewType":"NOT_SET","modified":"2025-08-31T18:39:21.7150190+00:00","id":20220001449,"sourceIdentifiers":[{"number":"2014/53053","submissionId":20220001449,"id":"acea1cc9e94549808081aa3a4