mber":"NNJ13HB90C","submissionId":20260003174,"id":"56f26ae337154367b3c19536798fada5","type":"CONTRACT_GRANT"},{"number":"NNJ14HD13P","submissionId":20260003174,"id":"e0b07ada306c46da8c2a7e188d9275b2","type":"CONTRACT_GRANT"},{"number":"NNX15CJ53C","submissionId":20260003174,"id":"1a2504728965470a80ea325a87e9cfea","type":"CONTRACT_GRANT"},{"number":"NNJ16HD82P","submissionId":20260003174,"id":"91586196b83249b19ae3765b682c9532","type":"CONTRACT_GRANT"},{"number":"NNJ17HD05P","submissionId":20260003174,"id":"d6e3bd087e564dedb82ed1266e5281a4","type":"CONTRACT_GRANT"},{"number":"80JSC018P0002","submissionId":20260003174,"id":"c5bb437310bb4e359104e95e309bd535","type":"CONTRACT_GRANT"},{"number":"80JSC019P0029","submissionId":20260003174,"id":"35e1c9ff37e845aab04f5487621878bf","type":"CONTRACT_GRANT"},{"number":"N6833518C0653","submissionId":20260003174,"id":"24375fa8afca4e629d1d6cad3089f168","type":"CONTRACT_GRANT"},{"number":"N6833519C0721","submissionId":20260003174,"id":"180666f33cb04f8e9a5ec927bd9115a1","type":"CONTRACT_GRANT"}],"title":"The Operational NASA Anomaly Gas Analyzer and Exploration Follow-Ons","stiType":"CONFERENCE_PAPER","distribution":"PUBLIC","submittedDate":"2026-04-16T18:37:32.0680570+00:00","authorAffiliations":[{"organizationId":"4c6a2b878d76521da887cdcb2564bf90","sequence":0,"submissionId":20260003174,"meta":{"author":{"name":"Nicholas R Wymore"},"organization":{"name":"Johnson Space Center","location":"Houston, United States"}},"id":"17863a1133ce4742b4515dbb9c5783b4","userType":"CIVIL","userId":"2c6521ea3319411a919bb8d96db1ef8a"},{"organizationId":"4c6a2b878d76521da887cdcb2564bf90","sequence":1,"submissionId":20260003174,"meta":{"author":{"orcidId":"","name":"Brian P Rodrigue"},"organization":{"name":"Johnson Space Center","location":"Houston, United States"}},"id":"d4614881afd542df9f94a59f4b0cff4b","userType":"CIVIL","userId":"4ee595db14c7490388299e9a57200bda"},{"organizationId":"9074213324715ab2856abb189582d04e","sequence":2,"submissionId":20260003174,"meta":{"author":{"orcidId":"","name":"Jeffrey S Pilgrim"},"organization":{"name":"Vista Photonics (United States)","location":"Las Cruces, United States"}},"id":"e3bceb938c724edda73edfd069ede9a2","userType":"OTHER","userId":"36ce15d317bf497c8d1dc16ff02af366"}],"stiTypeDetails":"Conference Paper","technicalReviewType":"EXTERNAL_PEER_COMMITTEE","modified":"2026-04-22T11:55:30.1587330+00:00","id":20260003174,"created":"2026-04-16T18:26:59.2564800+00:00","center":{"code":"JSC","name":"Johnson Space Center","id":"dd46994b91a94cb2a2722617cbbadc55"},"onlyAbstract":false,"sensitiveInformation":2,"abstract":"The NASA Anomaly Gas Analyzer (AGA) is the culmination of nearly ten years of advancement of core technology originally developed by Vista Photonics through the Small Business Innovation Research (SBIR) program and expanded using NASA program funding. The AGA is a portable, battery operated, optical gas detection instrument for continuous monitoring of H<sub>2</sub>O, CO<sub>2</sub>, O<sub>2</sub>, CO, NH<sub>3</sub>, HCN, HF, HCl in the spacecraft environment. Real time, in-situ monitored, gas concentrations are communicated at 1 Hz through a built-in display on-orbit and additionally through a serial interface on the ground. The instruments function over the typical range of temperatures and pressures required for the spacecraft environment. A ten-year operational life is targeted. Seven AGA instruments are currently deployed on the International Space Station and two Orion-specific units are manifested for Artemis 2. A variation of the operational AGA is under development for lunar Human Landing Systems. A wider calibrated operating pressure envelope is required in this application. Otherwise, the instruments straightforwardly address lessons learned from the original AGA by improving aspects of thermal control, ruggedness, and ease-of-use. A subset of AGA-developed sensed gases (H<sub>2</sub>O, CO<sub>2</sub>, O<sub>2</sub>) is the subject of the Primary Constituent Monitor (PCM) development for the Habitation and Logistics Outpost (HALO) destined for lunar orbit. Two PCMs will be installed in HALO as part of the environmental control loop where they will interface with vehicle power and communications. While the basic sensor techniques are unchanged from the AGA, these instruments have been radiation hardened to survive for many years in the harsh target environment. Current development status for these instruments will be presented along with recent developments of a naval submarine variant.","isLessonsLearned":false,"disseminated":"DOCUMENT_AND_METADATA","cui":{"isCui":false,"submissionId":20260003174,"id":"3a888e3308114b99ac6751cebb1ba4e5"},"meetings":[{"country":"PR","submissionId":20260003174,"endDate":"2026-07-16T05:00:00.0000000+00:00","sponsors":[{"organizationId":"8a4c6bb4d92d4b57b245023ae13c7536","meta":{"organization":{"name":"International Conference on Environmental Systems","location":"Emmaus, Pennsylvania, United States"}},"meetingId":"f191734dadad4d4d834769d9f2394606","id":"8589e191de1940c29715c6ab78a44d34"}],"name":"55th International Conference on Environmental Systems (ICES)","location":"Rio Grande","id":"f191734dadad4d4d834769d9f2394606","url":"https://www.ices.space","startDate":"2026-07-12T05:00:00.0000000+00:00"}],"publications":[{"submissionId":20260003174,"publisher":"International Conference on Environmental Systems","id":"8f407a1ddb9a4601b740c78366c6b01b","publicationDate":"2026-07-12T04:00:00.0000000+00:00"}],"status":"CURATED","related":[],"downloads":[{"draft":false,"mimetype":"application/pdf","name":"AGA Paper with ICES Header post Review.pdf","notes":"False positives. The author did not add the trade names statement to the document, but that is not an STI requirement, so the paper is fine as is. This is why I did not resolve the comment.","type":"STI","links":{"original":"/api/citations/20260003174/downloads/AGA%20Paper%20with%20ICES%20Header%20post%20Review.pdf","pdf":"/api/citations/20260003174/downloads/AGA%20Paper%20with%20ICES%20Header%20post%20Review.pdf","fulltext":"/api/citations/20260003174/downloads/AGA%20Paper%20with%20ICES%20Header%20post%20Review.pdf.txt"}}],"downloadsAvailable":true,"index":"submissions-2026-11-12-06-24"},{"_meta":{"score":63.710163},"copyright":{"thirdPartyPermissionsProduced":false,"disclosedToPublic":false,"containsIndication":false,"publisherPermissionOrRightsToDistribute":false,"belongsToUsGov":false,"determinationType":"GOV_PUBLIC_USE_PERMITTED","thirdPartyContentCondition":"NOT_SET","belongsToContractor":false,"disclosedInvention":false,"submissionId":20050217459,"containsThirdPartyMaterial":false,"belongsToPublisher":false,"id":"3c90aa88aee646d6858158c4b97786e7","belongsToAuthors":false},"subjectCategories":["Spacecraft Instrumentation And Astrionics"],"exportControl":{"isExportControl":"NO","submissionId":20050217459,"ear":"NO","id":"06de35e6e379441a86c0f728e383d9d2","itar":"NO"},"distributionDate":"2019-07-17T00:00:00.0000000+00:00","title":"Development and Performance of the Oxygen Sensor in the CSA-CP Aboard the International Space Station","stiType":"ABSTRACT","distribution":"PUBLIC","submittedDate":"2013-08-23T12:18:00.0000000+00:00","authorAffiliations":[{"sequence":0,"submissionId":20050217459,"meta":{"author":{"name":"Limero, Thomas"},"organization":{"name":"Wyle Labs., Inc.","location":"Houston, TX, United States"}},"id":"0a18eeaa44d449488879c0e6eb1545e2"},{"sequence":1,"submissionId":20050217459,"meta":{"author":{"name":"Beck, Steve"},"organization":{"name":"Wyle Labs., Inc.","location":"Houston, TX, United States"}},"id":"a9fa490c15fa41058b71502aed5d16df"},{"sequence":2,"submissionId":20050217459,"meta":{"author":{"name":"James, John T."},"organization":{"name":"NASA Johnson Space Center","location":"Houston, TX, United States"}},"id":"c08761045272405cb973852d78ea3125"}],"stiTypeDetails":"Abstract","technicalReviewType":"TECHNICAL_REVIEW_TYPE_NONE","modified":"2025-08-31T18:39:21.7150190+00:00","id":20050217459,"legacyMeta":{"__type":"LegacyMetaIndex, StrivesApi.ServiceModel","accessionNumber":""},"created":"2013-08-23T12:18:00.0000000+00:00","center":{"code":"JSC","name":"Johnson Space Center","id":"dd46994b91a94cb2a2722617cbbadc55"},"onlyAbstract":true,"sensitiveInformation":2,"abstract":"A combustion products analyzer (CPA) was built for use on Shuttle in response to several thermodegradation incidents that had occurred during early flights. The CPA contained sensors that measured carbon monoxide, hydrogen chloride, hydrogen cyanide, and hydrogen fluoride. These marker compounds, monitored by the CPA, were selected based upon the likely products to be released in a spacecraft fire. When the Toxicology Laboratory group at Johnson Space Center (JSC) began to assess the air quality monitoring needs for the International Space Station (ISS), the CPA was the starting point for design of an instrument to monitor the atmosphere following a thermodegradation event. The final product was significantly different from the CPA and was named the compound specific analyzer-combustion products (CSA-CP). The major change from the CPA that will be the focus of this paper was the replacement of an unreliable hydrogen fluoride (HF) sensor with an oxygen sensor. A reliable HF sensor was not commercially available, but as the toxicology group reviewed the overall monitoring strategy for ISS, it appeared that a portable oxygen sensor to backup the major constituent analyzer was needed. Therefore, an oxygen sensor replaced the HF sensor in the new instrument. This paper will describe the development, deployment, and performance of the CSA-CP oxygen sensor on both Shuttle and ISS. Also, data for CSA-CP oxygen sensor accuracy at nominal and reduced pressures will be presented.","isLessonsLearned":false,"disseminated":"METADATA_ONLY","meetings":[{"country":"United States","submissionId":20050217459,"endDate":"2004-07-22T00:00:00.0000000+00:00","sponsors":[{"organizationId":"148f